{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 14, :comments ["<b>You shall not curse the deaf.</b> I have only the deaf; from where do I include every person? Scripture says: \"among your people you shall not curse\" (Exodus 22:27). If so, why is the deaf stated? The deaf is uniquely distinguished in that he is alive, thus excluding the dead, who is not alive — this is the language of Rashi (Rashi on Leviticus 19:14). And this is taught in Torat Kohanim (parashah 2, chapter 13). But the midrash in the Gemara (Sanhedrin 66a) is not so; rather, Scripture warned concerning the dignitaries of the people — the judge and the prince — as it says (Exodus 22:27), \"Elohim you shall not curse, and a prince among your people you shall not curse,\" and then it turned back and warned (here) concerning the lowly of the people, which is the deaf, and from them one learns by way of a general principle to all the rest of the people, for from the first to the last, all are included in the prohibition. And the phrase \"among your people\" is expounded (in Sanhedrin 85a) — to one who does the deeds of your people, to exclude the wicked. And according to the way of the plain sense, Scripture mentioned the deaf in the matter of cursing because even though he will not hear and will not take offense at the curse, the Torah warned concerning him, and it is unnecessary to say this concerning those who hear, who will be ashamed and become very angry. Furthermore, it warns concerning a real-life situation: a person curses the deaf and places a stumbling block before the blind, because he does not fear them since they do not know and do not understand; therefore, \"and you shall fear your Elohim\" (here), for He sees what is hidden. And He added another prohibition concerning those in authority — the prince and the judge — because it is the way of people to curse them in the inner chambers of their beds, when in judgment a wicked man is condemned; and in the cursing of the prince and the judge there are many pitfalls, for the multitudes of the people in their folly hate them and will be roused to rise up against them, while they through their judgment establish the land."], :book :leviticus, :voice :ramban, :lang :en, :chapter 19, :notes {0 "The phrase בקללה is read as 'in the matter of cursing.' The word תקלות (pitfalls/dangers) is taken literally."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T03:55:56.006193561Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}